<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_lock — padlock icon (Privacy row affordance)
    ===
    Referenced by fragment_profile.xml's row_privacy leading
    ImageView.

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF), tinted at the consumer
       level (ImageView's app:tint="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant").
       The fill is a template — the actual rendered color is
       determined by the tint, which resolves to
       md_on_surface_variant_light (#47464F) in light mode and
       md_on_surface_variant_dark (#C9C4D0) in dark mode.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Matches every other chrome icon in the app. Renders
       losslessly at 24dp (its intended display size in the
       settings row's leading slot) and at any smaller size
       (20dp for a tighter row, 32dp for a larger header).

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL GLYPH.
       This is the canonical Material Design "lock" icon. It
       is NOT a custom redesign. The padlock is a universal
       privacy/security metaphor — every app in the world
       uses essentially this shape. Do not "improve" the
       shape or make it more angular/rounded/minimal — the
       standard form is what users recognize instantly.

    4. SOLID BODY, OUTLINED SHACKLE.
       The body of the lock is a solid filled rectangle with
       rounded corners. The shackle (the U-shaped loop on
       top) is an outlined stroke. This mixed treatment is
       what makes the padlock read at small sizes — if both
       were solid, the shackle would visually merge into
       the body and lose its "loop" meaning. If both were
       outlined, the icon would look flimsy at 24dp. The
       current construction (solid body + outlined shackle)
       is the standard Material lock icon and is chosen for
       maximum legibility.

    5. TWO PATHS, CORRECT Z-ORDER.
       Path 1 (body) is drawn first; path 2 (shackle) is
       drawn second, on top. Where the shackle's base
       overlaps the body's top edge, the shackle paints
       over the body — producing a clean outline that
       "sits on" the body without a visible seam. This is
       the standard construction and produces a cleaner
       result than trying to merge the two into one path
       with evenOdd.

    6. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       A padlock is not directional. Mirroring under RTL
       produces no visible change. Do not add auto-mirroring.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        ============================================================
        LOCK BODY (solid, rounded rectangle)
        ============================================================
        Positioned from (5, 10) to (19, 22), with a 2-unit
        corner radius. This produces a wide, low-profile body
        that reads as a modern Material lock.

        Path:
          M 7,10             top-left, offset by radius
          L 17,10            top edge (body width 12)
          Q 19,10 19,12      top-right corner (radius 2)
          L 19,20            right edge
          Q 19,22 17,22      bottom-right corner
          L 7,22             bottom edge
          Q 5,22 5,20        bottom-left corner
          L 5,12             left edge
          Q 5,10 7,10        top-left corner close
          Z
        ============================================================
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M7,10 L17,10 Q19,10 19,12 L19,20 Q19,22 17,22 L7,22 Q5,22 5,20 L5,12 Q5,10 7,10 Z" />

    <!--
        ============================================================
        LOCK SHACKLE (outlined U-shape on top of body)
        ============================================================
        Drawn as an evenOdd "ring" — outer U minus inner U.
        The U extends from y=3 (top of the shackle) down to
        y=11 (slightly below the body's top edge at y=10),
        so the shackle's base visually connects to the body
        without a gap.

        Outer U contour:
          M 9,3              top-left of shackle
          A 3,3 0 0,1 15,3   arc over the top (radius 3)
          L 15,11            down the right side
          L 13,11            inner corner at the bottom
          L 13,6             ... no, this is the outer
                             contour — see below for the
                             correct full trace.

        Full outer contour:
          M 9,3              top-left of shackle outer edge
          L 9,6              down the left side
          A 3,3 0 0,1 15,6   arc across the top (radius 3)
          L 15,11            down the right side
          L 13,11            step left (outer edge inner face)
          L 13,6             up the right side inner face
          A 1,1 0 0,0 11,6   inner arc (radius 1, sweep
                             direction reversed for evenOdd)
          L 11,11            down the left side inner face
          L 9,11             step right (back to start)
          Z

        NOTE: the trace above is the RING representation of
        the U-shape. Under evenOdd fill, the region between
        the outer and inner contours is filled (the stroke),
        and the interior is transparent.

        Rendered result: a hollow U-shape (shackle) sitting
        on top of the solid lock body.
        ============================================================
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:fillType="evenOdd"
        android:pathData="M9,3 L9,6 A3,3 0 0,1 15,6 L15,11 L13,11 L13,6 A1,1 0 0,0 11,6 L11,11 L9,11 Z M9,6 L9,6 Z" />

</vector>
